# SYNOPSIS

```usage
uniq [-c] [-d | -u] [-f fields] [-s chars] [input [output]]
```

# DESCRIPTION

`uniq` copies <input>, or standard input, to <output>, or standard output, writing one line of each run of equal adjacent lines.
An <input> or <output> of `-` is standard input or output.
Lines that are equal but not adjacent are not a run: sort(1) the input first to find every repeat.

: `-c`
  Write before each line the number of lines in its run, right-aligned in 7 columns and followed by a space.
: `-d`
  Write only the lines that were repeated.
: `-u`
  Write only the lines that were not.
: `-f` <fields>
  Ignore the first <fields> fields of each line when comparing; a field is blanks then non-blanks.
: `-s` <chars>
  Ignore the first <chars> bytes when comparing, after any fields `-f` skips.

`uniq` is sbase's, built into `sbase-box` with sbase's other commands and installed as `/boot/bin/posix/uniq`, a hard link to it.
In the POSIX namespace (a manifest's `ns=posix`, made by `/lib/ns/posix`, see namespace(6)) `/boot/bin/posix` is bound before `/boot/bin` on `/bin`, so POSIX programs find it as `/bin/uniq`.
There is no native `uniq`.

# EXAMPLES

sbase's test (`tests/posix/sbasetest.c`) runs `uniq -c` with the lines `a`, `a` and `b` on standard input, and gets:

```text
      2 a
      1 b
```

# SEE ALSO

sort(1), comm(1); sbase's page is `third_party/sbase/uniq.1` in the repository

# DIAGNOSTICS

An <input> or <output> that cannot be opened is reported as `uniq: fopen` <file>`:` and the error, and `uniq` exits 1.
A read or write error, a bad number and the usage message exit 1 too.

# BUGS

`-s` counts bytes, where POSIX counts characters.

The newline is part of the comparison, so a last line without one differs from the same text with one.

`-d` with `-u` is accepted, and writes nothing.

<output> is opened, and truncated, before <input> is read, so naming one file twice loses it.

Only `uniq -c` has been run on VectraOS; the rest is untested here.
